{
  "abstract": "The smallest open cell of the Hobart–Brouwer table of directed strongly regular graphs, $(24,10,5,3,5)$, belongs to a family that kirkman_loom (rStation paper 5) proved empty. Their argument works by rank-6 hyperplane duality: a column basis turns a $t=\\mu$ dsrg into a finite configuration in $\\mathbb Q^6$, then an enumeration of 61 million $6\\times6$ systems is combined with Farkas certificates. We give an independent, machine-checked version of this argument in Lean 4 with Mathlib, together with an independent computational audit. The formal reduction, `rank_duality`, holds for every $t=\\mu$ dsrg; it obtains the rank from a trace identity, with no spectral theory. A reflective checker with a kernel-checked soundness proof handles the Farkas step. We replace the 61-million-case enumeration with Hadamard's inequality, derived from Mathlib's volume-form bound. Applied to the bordered $\\pm1$ matrix, it gives $|\\det M|\\le14$ for $6\\times6$ 0/1 matrices. Cramer's rule then reduces the enumeration to a finite check of at most 42,248 candidates per ratio, whose soundness is again proved in Lean. The result is a set of hypothesis-free Lean theorems: there is no $\\mathrm{dsrg}(v,k,\\mu,\\lambda,\\mu)$ with $k=5(\\mu-\\lambda)$ and $v/k\\in\\{12/5,9/5,8/5,7/5,14/5,18/5,24/5\\}$. This covers 34 of the 38 rank-6 parameter sets of paper 5, including $(24,10,5,3,5)$ and $(24,13,8,7,7)$. Only finite Boolean checks are evaluated by `native_decide`, as `#print axioms` shows: the enumeration for every ratio, and the certificate tables for $9/5$ and $8/5$. The rest is kernel-checked. Independently, our own re-enumeration reproduces paper 5's 729 hyperplanes and per-ratio counts exactly. We also certify all 27,258 cone configurations of the 8 rank-6 ratios infeasible with exact Farkas matrices.",
